patch=1,EE,00170374,word,0816D080
// ===== Ground: Caim always faces the stick direction exactly (the walk turn step is replaced) =====
// The walk turn step of FUN_00147560 turned only 0.125 of the angle to the chase frame per frame and (as the angle is derived from the chase frame, not from the stick) lagged badly when the stick was swung round (a 360 sweep). Two parts. (1) SEL (005B2A00; the ori at 001475B8 becomes j SEL, the dsll32 behind it runs in the delay slot and is redone): for the call of the free walk (saved return address 00170370) the double constant is 0.0, so the walk adds no turn of its own; other callers keep 0.125. (2) TURNC (005B2B00; the daddu s2,a0,zero at 0016FBA0 in the prologue of the ground handler FUN_0016FB98 becomes j TURNC, its delay slot, the sd s4, stays, TURNC redoes the daddu and jumps to 0016FBA8): when the stick (shorts +0x67C x, +0x67E y at a1+0x18 / +0x1A, in Caims frame, forward = -y, right = +x) is at least 0x400 from the centre and the block action is not held it computes delta = atan2(x, -y) (polynomial from the lock-on cave, error under 0.001 rad) and does what the lock-on routine FACE does: [+0x204] = delta (yaw accumulator), [+0x210] |= 2, [+0x584] = (yaw [+0x44] + delta) wrapped to 0..2 pi (the chase frame yaw), [+0x570] |= 1 (rebuild). So Caim is turned by the full angle between his facing and the stick every frame: his facing follows the stick through any sweep. DEBUG: every call writes a 32 byte record into a ring buffer (index 005B3100, 128 records from 005B3110: stick x, -y, delta, target T, actual turn, actual x, actual z, frame number) to be read from a save state. Version 8 (the double image at super sprint): the facing log of a glitchy state showed the actual turn dActual = T - yaw alternating +0.131 / -0.131 EVERY frame with T constant, i.e. the actual yaw jumps from T-0.131 to T+0.131 and back (two faded Caims at 60 fps): the yaw update applied for [+0x204] has a gain of about 2 per frame (e1 = e0 * (1 - g), g = 2 gives a perfectly alternating error that any noise makes grow, so it appeared slowly after a while of straight running). The turn written to [+0x204] is now half of T - yaw: with g = 2 the actual yaw reaches T at once, with g = 1 it still converges (e halves per frame). Version 7: the log showed the chase frame yaw [+0x584] creeping back to its old value every frame: FUN_0012A624, which I called to rebuild, is the matrix -> euler direction (the game calls it when bit 0 of [+0x570] is set), so it overwrote my yaw with the old matrix yaw and the forward row used for the direction stayed old. The rebuild now sets bit 1 (euler changed) and calls FUN_0012A644 (euler -> matrix, which the game calls when bit 1 is set). Version 6: the chase frame POSITION is no longer touched (moving it each frame swung the camera, which follows it, and the camera-relative stick then rotated every frame: the log showed the stick angle jumping by about 90 degrees per frame). Instead TURNC sets a flag (005B2FC0) and the constant speed cave NORM (now at 005B4200, hook 00170374) uses the chase frame forward row (+0x5B0 / +0x5B8) as the direction of the displacement while the flag is set, with the length f20 * |stick| / 4096: Caim moves along the stick direction at once. Version 5 (free movement, withdrawn): the body is pulled towards the chase frame POSITION (+0x5C0 / +0x5C8, about 70-100 units ahead of Caim at +0x80 / +0x88), which stayed on the old heading line, so a quick heading change gave a huge turning radius. After the rebuild the cave puts the chase frame position on the new heading line (Caim position + new forward row +0x5B0 / +0x5B8 times the same distance, at most 150). Version 4 (jitter): after writing the chase frame yaw the cave rebuilds the chase frame at once (FUN_0012A624 on +0x570; ra, a0-a2 and the length are kept in 005B2F90..A0) and replaces the stick by (0, -length) with length = sqrt(x^2 + y^2): the stick is now exactly forward in the new frame, so the walk of the same frame moves along the stick direction, and a second call in the same frame (the handler can run more than once per pad update) finds angle 0 instead of turning again. Deadband: an angle under 0.05 rad (about 3 degrees) counts as 0 for the chase frame (stick noise), the actual yaw is still synchronised to it. Version 3: the stick shorts are in the frame of the CHASE frame (+0x570, matrix rows at +0x580..; its yaw is [+0x584], its right vector +0x590 = (cos, -sin), so x > 0 = positive yaw, sign as first written), not in the actual frame of Caim, which lags behind it; the first two versions added the angle to the actual yaw and spun. Now T = wrap([+0x584] + delta) is written back to [+0x584] and the actual turn [+0x204] = wrap(T - yaw [+0x44]), so both frames end on the same heading and nothing accumulates. This section replaces the earlier sections that tune the turn (the turn-around assist and the 0.125 -> 1.0 constant): untick them.
patch=1,EE,005B2A00,word,8FA80058
