// ===== Lock-on: target single soldiers instead of squad centres (changes the game's nearest-enemy search; needs Lock-on core) =====
// Hook 0016FAD4 inside the game's nearest-enemy search FUN_0016FA38 (candidate state load -> cave 005B8140): the search mostly found SQUAD objects (halfword +0x24 = 1, position = squad centre); the cave rejects every candidate with +0x24 == 1 and accepts all others regardless of their state (soldiers are type 6, cavalry 11, state 5..7 while reacting to a hit), so the lock sits on one soldier. NOTE: FUN_0016FA38 is a game function and this hook is active ALWAYS (not only while locked), so every game caller of the search now skips squads (prime suspect for enemies not spawning / quests breaking).
patch=1,EE,0016FAD4,word,0816E050
patch=1,EE,005B8140,word,946B0024
patch=1,EE,005B8144,word,396B0001
patch=1,EE,005B8148,word,0805BEB7
patch=1,EE,005B814C,word,000B102B
// ===== Lock-on: dot over the target and camera keeps it in view (needs the R3 toggle and Lock-on core) =====
// 2026-10-30: the lock-on dot fill is red, the shade of the compass enemy dots (screenshot peak 218 / 2 / 1; RGBAQ word 005A01A0 = 800102DA, was 80FFFFFF white). // 2026-10-30: the lock-on dot is half its former size: every halfword of the two shape tables (005A0400..005A0444 black outline, 005A0480..005A04C4 white fill; 1/16 px offsets from the dot centre) was halved.
// Lock-on dot and camera rule: hook 0012732C (ld s1,8(sp) near the end of FUN_001270E8, after FUN_002a61d0 has linked the 2D-lib DMA chains) -> cave DOT at 003420B0. While locked it finds the nearest enemy (FUN_0016FA38, radius 400), projects it with the camera object 003ADAC0 (right row +0x50, down +0x60, forward +0x70, position +0x80; focal 632.6 vertical, 474.5 horizontal because of the 16:9 patch) and draws a black 10x12 plus white 6x8 sprite through a GIF packet at 004E0000 (static words are plain patch lines; DOT writes only the four vertex words). The packet is appended by turning the last head tag of the chain (base 004F50D0 + 0xA3*16) from END into a NEXT tag to the buffer; when not drawing the END tag is restored. Camera rule: if the dot would leave the diamond centred (317,190) (half width 60, half height 66; eased: gentle pull from 70 percent of the limit, hard pull beyond), DOT turns the camera by adding to the orbit yaw W (0013E538, yaw error = excess px * z/horizontal distance / 474.5 * 0.8, max 0.06 rad per frame) and to the camera pitch offset (+0x180, excess px / 632.6 * -0.6; flip the sign constant GY in the generator if the vertical correction goes the wrong way). The yaw gain uses the Caim-enemy horizontal distance (the orbit camera pivots around Caim). IMPORTANT: on the R5900 sqrt.s takes its source in the ft field (fs = 0); earlier builds encoded it the other way, so every sqrt in these caves read the wrong register. When no living enemy is within the radius the lock switches itself off (clears bit0 of 00321E2C). A target behind the camera swings the yaw 0.08 rad per frame.
// The GIF packet and the dot shape tables now live at 005A0000..005A02D0, 005A0400..005A0444 and 005A0480..005A04C4 (moved from 004E0000, which overlapped the voice buffer). The Angelus fireball reticle, smooth lock-on camera and 2x lock-on camera sections jump into DOT and need this section.
patch=1,EE,0012732C,word,080D082C
