// ===== Camera: eye height held on the view line (safe, capped correction) =====
// Safe version of the eye correction (the first try that replaced the whole eye detached the camera). The eye of FUN_00196DB4 ends up up to about 5 units above or below the line the camera looks along (view direction fixed, eye to aim angle 0.15 .. 0.23 rad at a fixed pitch of 0.186), so Caim slid slightly on the screen while he ran. 001974B8 (lui at,0x3F80 just before the final stores) becomes j EYEFIX (005B5E00, the mtc1 in the delay slot is redone). EYEFIX changes ONLY the eye height: target y = aim y - forward row y * distance (aim = [cam+0xE0], forward row y = [cam+0x74], distance = [cam+0x14C], reached relative to s1 = cam+0xE0), moves the eye towards it by at most 6 units and leaves x and z alone. It does nothing when the distance is outside 10..300, the eye is closer than 20 or farther than 200 units from the aim point (camera not set up yet) or the forward row y is outside -1..1, so the camera cannot detach. Camera collision still moves the eye sideways as before. Remove this section to get the game eye height back.
// Version 4: the cap is 70 units (was 8). Two states with the R3 lock-on (target died / was knocked away and the camera switched) showed the view pitch [cam+0x40] at -0.701 while the game eye was only 0.156 rad above the aim point (the game eye elevation lags / differs after the lock-on camera rule moves the pitch offset quickly), so Caim was no longer in the middle of the screen vertically although everything else matched. With the larger cap the eye is always put exactly at the elevation of the view pitch, whatever the game eye did; the horizontal direction (azimuth) is still the game one. Version 3: the debug record showed that at the hook the forward row [cam+0x70..] is still the one of the previous orientation (y = 0, rebuilt later in the frame), so the row based target pushed the eye 6 units the wrong way. EYEFIX now uses the pitch accumulator [cam+0x40] (set before by the pitch section): target vertical offset dy = distance * sin(cam40) (sine polynomial), applied to the eye height with a cap of 8 units, and the horizontal part is rescaled so that the eye stays exactly on the sphere of radius distance around the aim point; it skips the frame when the distance is outside 10..300, the eye is closer than 20 / farther than 200 units from the aim, the horizontal part is below 2 units or |cam40| is above 1.5. Needs the pitch (absolute pitch) section.
patch=1,EE,005BBC04,word,3C014120
patch=1,EE,005BBC08,word,34210000
patch=1,EE,005BBC0C,word,44815800
